+section('docker', 'Using Docker in a job', 'box-seam')
  p.
    The Client doesn't pull images or start containers itself and doesn't need Docker. A job that needs a container runs
    #[code docker] in its #[code --command], on a Client host that has Docker (the runner card's #[strong Capabilities] tab says
    whether it does). Registry credentials come from the job as #[code --env]: masked everywhere, dropped when the job ends.

  h3.h6 Log in, pull and run, credentials scoped to the job
  p.small.
    Point #[code DOCKER_CONFIG] at the job's directory so the login is deleted with the job instead of staying in the
    service user's #[code ~/.docker] for every later job:
  +code('Any job type').
    export DOCKER_PASSWORD=…        # from your CI secret store, never on the command line
    thub run --type sw \
      --env DOCKER_REGISTRY=registry.lab.local:5000,DOCKER_USER=ci --env DOCKER_PASSWORD \
      --env TARGET=staging \
      --command 'export DOCKER_CONFIG="$THUB_WORK_DIR/.docker" &&
                 echo "$DOCKER_PASSWORD" | docker login "$DOCKER_REGISTRY" -u "$DOCKER_USER" --password-stdin &&
                 docker run --rm \
                   -v "$THUB_WORK_DIR:/work" -w /work --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)" \
                   -e TARGET -e THUB_SUITE -e THUB_JOB_ID \
                   "$DOCKER_REGISTRY/team/test-runner:1.4" ./run-tests.sh --junit results/junit.xml' \
      --wait
  table.table.table-sm.small
    thead
      tr
        th docker run option
        th Why
    tbody
      tr
        td: code --rm
        td Removes the container when the tests end.
      tr
        td: code -v "$THUB_WORK_DIR:/work" -w /work
        td The work directory, where the Client reads JUnit XML under #[code results/] and #[code artifacts/] for the test counts.
      tr
        td: code -e NAME
        td Passes a job variable (#[code --env], #[code THUB_*], #[code JOB_*]) into the container.
      tr
        td: code --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)"
        td Files written to #[code /work] stay owned by the Client user, so the workspace can be cleaned up.
      tr
        td: code --device /dev/thub/dut1-uart
        td HW: gives the container the DUT's UART (#[code "$THUB_DUT_UART"]). Use #[code --privileged] only if you must.

  h3.h6 An emulator as the DUT
  p.small.
    Start it in the background in the command, talk to it, and stop it whatever the result:
  +code('SW job with an emulator container').
    thub run --type sw \
      --download-file https://artifactory.example.com/fw-local/app/1.4.0-42/app.elf \
      --env DOCKER_REGISTRY=registry.lab.local:5000,DOCKER_USER=ci --env DOCKER_PASSWORD \
      --command 'export DOCKER_CONFIG="$THUB_WORK_DIR/.docker" &&
                 echo "$DOCKER_PASSWORD" | docker login "$DOCKER_REGISTRY" -u "$DOCKER_USER" --password-stdin &&
                 dut="thub-$THUB_JOB_ID" && trap "docker rm -f $dut >/dev/null" EXIT &&
                 docker run -d --name "$dut" -p 127.0.0.1:5555:5555 -v "$THUB_DOWNLOADS_DIR:/downloads:ro" \
                   "$DOCKER_REGISTRY/dut-emulator:2026.08" &&
                 ./ci/test.sh --dut 127.0.0.1:5555' \
      --wait
  +note('info').
    Docker access is effectively root access on that host. The service user needs the #[code docker] group: install Docker
    before the Client (#[code sudo npm i -g …] adds the group), or re-run the install afterwards.
